{"study_accession":"PRJNA403862","secondary_study_accession":"SRP117184","title":"Human primary macrophages derived in vitro from circulating monocytes comprise adherent and non-adherent subsets with differential expression of Siglec-1 and CD4 and permissiveness to HIV-1 infection","description":"Macrophages are a major target for human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) infection. However, macrophages are largely heterogeneous and may exhibit differences in permissiveness to HIV-1 infection. This study highlights the interplay of macrophage heterogeneity in HIV-1 pathogenesis. We show that monocyte-derived macrophages (MDM) could be divided into two distinct subsets: CD14+Siglec-1hiCD4+ (non-adherent MDM), and CD14+Siglec-1LoCD4- (adherent MDM). The CD14+Siglec-1hiCD4+MDM subset represented the smaller proportion in the macrophage pool, and varied among different donors. Fractionation and subsequent exposure of the two MDM subsets to HIV-1 revealed opposite outcomes in terms of HIV-1 capture and infection. Although the CD14+Siglec-1hiCD4+MDM captured significantly more HIV-1, infection was significantly higher in the CD14+Siglec-1LoCD4-MDM subset. Thus, CD14+Siglec-1hiCD4+MDM were less permissive to infection. Depletion of CD14+Siglec-1hiCD4+MDM or a decrease in their percentage, resulted in increased infection of MDM, suggestive of a capacity of these cells to capture and sequester HIV-1 in an environment that hinders its infectivity. Increased expression of innate restriction factors and cytokine genes were observed in the non-adherent CD14+Siglec-1hiCD4+MDM, both before and after HIV-1 infection, compared to the adherent CD14+Siglec-1LoCD4-MDM. The differential expression of gene expression profiles in the two macrophage subsets may provide an explanation for the differences observed in HIV-1 infectivity. Overall design: Adherent and non-adherant MDM that were either uninfected or infected with HIV-1 from 3 different PBMC donors were evaluated.","pubmed":"29123518","geo_series":"","n_samples":12,"study_labels":[],"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN07626339","study_accession":"PRJNA403862","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"d130_non-adherent_us1_infected.CTTGGAA","description":"d130_non-adherent_us1_infected.CTTGGAA","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM2778757","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2018-02-23","attributes":[["source_name","monocyte-derived macrophage"],["tissue","monocyte-derived macrophage"],["phenotype","Siglec-1 hi CD4+"],["hiv-1 infection status","Infected"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR6023111","experiment_accession":"SRX3173862","library_layout":"SINGLE","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 3000","read_count":25102955,"base_count":1255147750,"first_public":"2018-02-23","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 3000 sequencing: GSM2778757: d130_non-adherent_us1_infected.CTTGGAA Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN07626340","study_accession":"PRJNA403862","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"d130_non-adherent_uninfected.AAACCTT","description":"d130_non-adherent_uninfected.AAACCTT","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM2778756","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2018-02-23","attributes":[["source_name","monocyte-derived macrophage"],["tissue","monocyte-derived macrophage"],["phenotype","Siglec-1 hi CD4+"],["hiv-1 infection status","Uninfected"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR6023110","experiment_accession":"SRX3173861","library_layout":"SINGLE","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 3000","read_count":39935145,"base_count":1996757250,"first_public":"2018-02-23","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 3000 sequencing: GSM2778756: d130_non-adherent_uninfected.AAACCTT Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN07626341","study_accession":"PRJNA403862","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"d130_adherent_us1